.TH SHROODLER 1 "2026-09-05" "shroodler 0.2.0" "Shroodler Manual"
.SH NAME
shroodler \- web attack-surface mapping and payload-testing toolkit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shroodler
[\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-\-debug\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shroodler
crawls a web target, extracts passive security-relevant findings (headers,
cookies, secrets, CORS, GraphQL/OpenAPI discovery, JWT hygiene), optionally
runs active payload packs against discovered forms (SQLi, XSS, SSTI,
path-traversal, SSRF, open-redirect, XXE), and renders results as JSON,
HTML, CSV, SARIF, or JUnit. It refuses to touch any host outside
127.0.0.1/localhost unless
.B \-\-allow\-external
is passed, and every finding is validated against a shared JSON schema so
its output can be consumed by other tools (CI pipelines, the bundled
report generator, or a future UI).
.PP
This CLI is the full product surface; the desktop app shells out to the
same commands. See
.I docs/cli-surface.md
in the project repository for exactly what is and isn't wired into the
desktop UI yet.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB\-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print full tracebacks on error instead of a one-line message.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B crawl \fIURL\fR
Crawl a target URL. See
.B CRAWL OPTIONS
below.
.TP
.B diff \fIFINDINGS\fR \fIEXPECTED\fR
Compare a crawl's findings JSON against a checked-in
.I expected_findings.json
baseline. With
.BR \-\-gate ,
exits non-zero only on findings not present in the baseline (CI mode);
without it, also fails on baseline findings that are missing from the
current scan.
.BI \-\-source\-root " DIR"
attributes each new finding (gate mode) to a source file/line by
grepping route-registration patterns under
.I DIR
(Flask/FastAPI/Bottle/Express/Django-style), and if it's a git repo, the
commit that last touched that line -- printed as "looks introduced by
routes/export.py:44 (commit ...)" in text output, or as GitHub Actions
.B ::error
annotations with
.B \-\-format github\-annotations .
Heuristic/best-effort, not a real router analysis.
.TP
.B report \fIFINDINGS\fR
Render a findings JSON document as
.BR html ", " csv ", " json ", " sarif ", " junit ", or " md / markdown
(\fB\-\-format\fR).
.TP
\fBbaseline\fR \fIFINDINGS\fR, \fBexpected\fR \fIFINDINGS\fR
Write an
.I expected_findings.json
baseline from a scan (maps pages/forms/findings to their
expected_* shape). Both names run the same command;
.B expected_not_found
negatives are left empty for you to add by hand.
.TP
.B ingest\-sessions \fISESSIONS.JSONL\fR
Turn a captured proxy session (JSON Lines) into a findings document, as if
it had been crawled directly.
.TP
.B tokens \fISESSIONS.JSONL\fR
Analyze password-reset/verification-token predictability from a captured
proxy session. A token is normally delivered out-of-band (email/SMS), so
this only sees one if a tester's browser, routed through the recording
proxy, actually visited the reset/verification link -- capture the same
flow multiple times (e.g. request several password resets) to get more
than one sample, since only 2+ samples let this test for a sequential or
low-entropy generation pattern rather than a weaker length-only estimate.
.TP
.B cadence
Print the recommended
.B crawl
/
.B payload
flags for a
.B \-\-tier
of
.BR pr
(passive,
.B \-\-profile safe ,
skip payloads),
.B nightly
(balanced + IDOR + default packs), or
.B weekly
(aggressive + adaptive payloads). Prints commands; does not scan.
Still local-only unless you add
.B \-\-allow\-external
yourself.
.TP
.B triage \fIHOSTS...\fR
Classify a program's host list before spending crawl budget. Passive
Certificate Transparency discovery (\fB\-\-discover\fR), DNS/CNAME
analysis (dead / dangling-CNAME takeover / third-party SaaS / resolves),
and one gentle HTTP probe per live host (redirect-alias, WAF-challenge,
SSO/auth-gated, live-content). Does not crawl or fire payloads.
Concurrency and request rate are bounded and clamped; a detected egress
proxy drops the default parallelism; a WAF challenge or HTTP 429 pauses
the rest of that DNS zone instead of fanning out into a block.
.B \-\-header
attaches per-program required headers (e.g. Bugcrowd's identifying
header);
.B \-\-no\-active
skips the HTTP probe entirely.
.B \-\-format hosts
(or
.BR \-\-hosts\-out )
emits the worth-crawling URL list for piping into
.BR crawl .
Local-only by default;
.B \-\-allow\-external
is required for remote hosts and for CT discovery (which contacts
crt.sh, not the target).
.TP
.B payload \fICRAWL.JSON\fR
Run the bundled YAML payload packs (SQLi, XSS, SSTI, path-traversal, SSRF,
open-redirect, XXE) against every form in a crawl's output.
.B \-\-allow\-external
is required to target a non-local host;
.B \-\-pack
adds extra pack files or directories;
.B \-\-plugin
loads a plugin directory (see
.B \-\-plugin
under
.BR crawl ).
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-oob\-host " HOST"
Your own out-of-band collaborator-style server (self-hosted Interactsh, an
oast.* instance, or any host you control that logs incoming requests). A
fresh random subdomain of it is used as
.B {{MARKER_HOST}}
in payloads each run. Shroodler cannot poll your server for you -- for
.B blind
packs (currently the parameter-entity XXE and OOB SSRF payloads), check
its logs afterward for the token recorded in the output's
.B oob_probes
list.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-policy
Refuse to run unless the target publishes a
.I .well\-known/scan\-policy.json
consent manifest declaring allow/deny scope and rate limits.
.TP
.BI \-\-policy\-file " PATH"
Use a local scan-policy.json instead of fetching one from the target.
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-log " PATH"
Append a JSONL audit trail of every active request the guardrail allowed
or blocked (scope/rate-limit decisions), each entry stamped with the
manifest's hash as proof of which consent grant governed it.
.TP
.B \-\-adaptive
On a baseline miss, retry once with an adapted payload (via
.B $SHROODLER_PAYLOAD_MUTATE_CMD
if set, else a built-in keyword-case/inline-comment mutator) instead of
giving up after the pack's own static payload. Still gated by the same
rate/blast-radius limits as every other request; never reports a
mutated match at confidence=confirmed.
.RE
.TP
.B authz\-diff \fIHIGHER_CRAWL.JSON\fR
Replay every page URL from a crawl done under one session (e.g. an admin
account) using a second, lower-privileged session's
.B \-\-cookie
/\fB\-\-header\fR. Flags a URL as broken access control if the lower-priv
session can still reach it while an anonymous request to the same URL was
denied. Accepts the same
.BR \-\-require\-policy / \-\-policy\-file / \-\-audit\-log
guardrail flags as
.B payload
(this replays real requests too).
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-higher\-priv\-marker " STRING"
A string uniquely identifying the higher-privilege account's own data
(an email, username, or record value only it should see; repeatable).
If the lower-priv session's response contains it, the lead is upgraded
to confidence=confirmed instead of merely "reachable".
.TP
.BI \-\-lower\-priv\-marker " STRING"
A string identifying the lower-priv account's own data (repeatable), to
rule out a
.B \-\-higher\-priv\-marker
match that's coincidentally also the requester's own identity.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-identity\-confirmation
Drop a lead entirely instead of reporting it at lower confidence when no
.B \-\-higher\-priv\-marker
was found in the response.
.RE
.TP
.B proxy \fIARGS...\fR
Forward arguments to the
.B shroodler\-proxy
intercepting-proxy binary (start, ca, replay).
.TP
\fBhistory record\fR \fIFINDINGS.JSON\fR, \fBhistory list\fR
Save a scan into local history (default
.IR ~/.shroodler/history ,
override with
.B \-\-history\-dir
or
.BR $SHROODLER_HISTORY_DIR ),
or list what has been recorded.
.TP
.B trend \fIOLDER\fR \fINEWER\fR
Diff findings between two scans (by history id or file path), reporting
which finding (id, url) pairs were introduced or resolved between them.
Unlike
.BR diff ,
this compares two arbitrary scans rather than gating against a static
baseline.
.B \-\-gate\-on\-severity\-increase
exits 1 if a finding present in both scans (same id+url, so not
"introduced") has a worse severity in the newer scan -- a same-key
regression
.B diff \-\-gate
can't see, since its baseline never records a severity to compare
against.
.B \-\-gate\-on\-waf\-coverage\-drop
exits 1 if the fraction of crawled pages showing a WAF/bot-mitigation
challenge dropped by more than
.B \-\-waf\-drop\-threshold
(default 0.2 = 20 percentage points) between the two scans -- "my own
protection silently got weaker" as a tracked regression, surfaced in
JSON output either way as
.BR waf_coverage_regression .
Does NOT fail the build when the two scans' page counts differ by more
than 2x (a low-confidence comparison by default; the regression is
still reported) unless
.B \-\-gate\-even\-if\-page\-count\-mismatch
is also passed.
.TP
.B ask \fIQUESTION\fR \fISCAN.JSON\fR
Ask a question over a scan/report ("show critical findings", "any secret
issues", "reachable without auth"). A small built-in keyword matcher
answers severity/category filters and summaries offline; add
.B \-\-since \fIOLDER_SCAN.JSON\fR
for "what's new"/"what got resolved" questions. Set
.B $SHROODLER_ASK_LLM_CMD
to an external program's path to delegate free-form questions to it
instead.
.TP
.B mcp\-server
Run an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server on stdio exposing
.BR scan_route ,
.BR check_idor ,
.BR reverify_fix ,
.BR diff_since_baseline ", and"
.B explain_finding
as tools an MCP-speaking coding agent can call directly, instead of
shelling out to this CLI and parsing text output. Active tools require
a scan-policy consent manifest by default.
.B \-\-list\-tools
prints the catalog (names, descriptions, input schemas) as JSON and
exits without starting the stdio loop.
.TP
.B audit\-verify \fIAUDIT_LOG.JSONL\fR
Replay a guardrail
.B \-\-audit\-log
and confirm its hash chain is intact (each entry chains to the previous
one's hash); reports which line, if any, was deleted, reordered, or
edited after the fact.
.TP
.B compare\-engines \fIPYTHON_CRAWL.JSON\fR \fIGO_CRAWL.JSON\fR
Merge two crawl JSON documents (nominally the same target, one from each
engine) into one findings list, tagging each with which engine(s)
independently reproduced it. Agreement between two independent
implementations is a confidence signal; disagreement isn't itself proof
of a false positive (one engine may simply lack a check the other has).
.TP
\fBticket file\fR \fIFINDINGS.JSON\fR, \fBticket sync\fR \fIFINDINGS.JSON\fR
Open GitHub issues for findings that would fail
.B diff \-\-gate
(or every visible finding if
.B \-\-baseline
is omitted), deduped by the same (id, path) key used everywhere else.
.B ticket sync
also closes issues recorded in
.B \-\-state
whose finding is gone from the current scan.
Dry-run by default: prints a JSON plan and does not call
.BR gh .
.B \-\-apply
invokes
.B gh issue create
/
.B gh issue close
and writes
.BR \-\-state
(default
.IR .shroodler\-tickets.json ).
.B \-\-owners
assigns matching findings; 
.B \-\-repo
is passed through to
.BR gh .
.TP
\fBsla apply\fR \fISCAN.JSON\fR
Resolves each finding's owner (from an
.B \-\-owners
ownership-rules file, same id/url glob format as
.IR .shroodlerignore )
and looks up its earliest appearance in recorded scan history (see
.BR "history record" ),
escalating its severity by one level in a new
.B sla_severity
field once its age exceeds the SLA budget for its original severity
(critical=2d, high=7d, medium=30d, low=90d, info=180d by default).
.B \-\-gate
exits 1 and lists every SLA-breached finding on stderr.
.TP
\fBsuppress expiring\fR
Lists suppression rules whose
.B expires
date falls within
.B \-\-days
(default 14) -- the input a scheduled job needs to open a PR nudging
someone to extend-with-justification or remove a rule before it lapses,
rather than only finding out after
.B diff
starts failing.
.B \-\-format github\-pr\-body
renders a ready-to-use Markdown PR body;
.B \-\-gate
exits 1 if any rule is expiring.
.TP
.B reverify \fIURL\fR \fIFINDING_ID\fR
Re-crawls exactly
.I URL
(and, unless
.BR \-\-no\-payloads ,
re-runs active payload packs against it), then reports whether
.I FINDING_ID
still appears there -- closed-loop remediate-and-reverify. Exits 0 only
when
.B verified_fixed
is true (the finding is actually gone from a fresh scan, not merely "a
patch was applied"). Accepts the same
.BR \-\-require\-policy / \-\-policy\-file / \-\-audit\-log
guardrail flags as
.B payload
for its active-payload re-run.
.TP
.B gen\-regression\-test \fIURL\fR \fIFINDING_ID\fR
Runs the same check as
.BR reverify ;
only when it confirms the finding is fixed does it write a pytest module
(to
.B \-\-output
or stdout) that re-checks the same (finding_id, url) pair via
`shroodler.reverify.reverify()` on every future test run. Refuses (exit
1, no file written) if the finding is still present, or if reverify's
result came with a warning (e.g.
.I URL
has no query string, so a GET-parameter finding may not have actually
been re-tested) --
.B \-\-force
generates the test anyway.
.TP
.B attack\-path \fIFINDINGS.JSON\fR
Correlates every finding with its URL path-depth (a heuristic proxy for
click-distance from the target root -- path segment count, NOT a real
crawl-link-graph traversal, since neither engine persists one) and
whether the same scan found evidence of a guessable session/reset
token, rendering either JSON or a
.B \-\-format markdown
narrative report. E.g. "this XSS is reachable ~2 clicks from the root;
this scan also found a guessable reset token" -- a different priority
signal than either finding read alone.
.TP
.B self\-scan
Adversarial self-scan: renders synthetic hostile findings (classic
HTML/JS-injection payloads, CSV formula-injection triggers) through
every report renderer this tool ships and checks whether any of them
echoed the payload back unsafely -- unescaped HTML in
.BR html / markdown
output, broken well-formedness in
.BR sarif / junit
output, or an unneutralized formula trigger in
.B csv
output. Exits 1 if any renderer failed. Catches "the tool's own report
becomes a stored-XSS/CSV-injection vector for content the scanned
target controls" -- a real, semi-famous scanner bug class.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CRAWL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Apply a bundle of sane defaults for depth/max-pages/max-time/
check-rate-limit:
.BR safe ", " balanced " (default-equivalent), or " aggressive .
Any flag given explicitly on the command line still overrides the
profile's value for that setting.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
.B static
(default) or
.B headless
(real browser, for SPAs).
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " N"
Maximum crawl depth;
.B \-1
for unbounded.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-pages " N"
Stop starting new fetches after this many pages (default 400).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-time " SECONDS"
Wall-clock budget for the crawl; 0 means no limit.
.TP
\fB\-\-output\fR, \fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR
Write output to
.I FILE
instead of stdout.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT"
.BR json " (default), " html ", " csv ", " sarif ", or " junit .
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-robots
Ignore robots.txt disallow rules.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sitemap
Skip robots.txt Sitemap: and /sitemap.xml discovery seeds.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-external
Allow crawling a non-local target (any host outside
127.0.0.1/localhost). Off by default \(em only use this against hosts
you are authorized to test.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-rate\-limit
Fire repeated bad-credential requests at discovered login/auth forms to
check for missing rate limiting. Off by default: this sends real repeated
requests with real consequences (lockouts, alerting) against a live
target. Repeated requests to the same endpoint can also trip a WAF/
bot-mitigation challenge on the target mid-scan \(em see the
.B waf-challenge
finding category and
.B \-\-user\-agent
below if that happens.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-check\-rate\-limit
Force rate-limit probing off, even if
.B \-\-profile
.B aggressive
would otherwise enable it.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-idor
For crawled URLs with a purely-numeric path segment or query value,
replay adjacent IDs (n-1, n+1) using the crawl's own session and flag
ones returning a same-shaped JSON object. This is a lead worth manually
confirming, not a proven IDOR: a single session cannot tell whether the
adjacent ID belongs to a different account or is simply another of the
current account's own sequentially-allocated records, so expect noise on
targets where that's common. Off by default: this makes real extra GET
requests for IDs never organically discovered, potentially reading
another user's data on a vulnerable target, and a GET is not guaranteed
side-effect-free on every API. JSON API responses only.
.TP
.BI \-\-header " 'Name: value'"
Extra request header, repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-agent " STRING"
Override the User-Agent sent on every crawl request. Some targets serve
different content, or block requests entirely, based on User-Agent.
.TP
.BI \-\-cookie " name=value"
Cookie applied before the crawl, repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-cookie\-jar " FILE"
Netscape cookies.txt, JSON cookie list, or Playwright storageState.
.TP
.BI \-\-storage\-state " FILE"
Playwright storageState JSON (cookies only).
.TP
.BI \-\-login\-recipe " FILE"
JSON
.RI { url ", " method ", " fields }
posted once before crawling; merges hidden form fields. May also declare
.BR logout_url / logout_method / protected_url
to enable session-fixation and logout-invalidation checks.
If a later fetch returns 401 or a login redirect, the recipe is re-run
once and that URL is retried (capped at one re-auth per crawl).
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy " URL"
Route crawl traffic through an HTTP proxy, e.g. the bundled
.BR shroodler\-proxy .
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " URL"
Extra same-origin URL to enqueue, repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-spec " FILE"
Local OpenAPI/Swagger document or Postman collection; same-origin paths
are enqueued as extra crawl seeds. Repeatable. Does not fetch the spec
from the target (crawl already probes
.I /openapi.json
on its own).
.TP
.BI \-\-seed\-from " FILE"
Seed URLs from a captured proxy session JSONL.
.TP
.BI \-\-cookies\-from " FILE"
Cookies from a captured proxy session JSONL.
.TP
.BI \-\-plugin " PATH"
Load a plugin directory or file (repeatable). A plugin supplies extra
payload packs, secret-detection rules, and/or a trusted Python
.B check()
hook. Also reads
.BR $SHROODLER_PLUGIN_PATH
(colon-separated). Same flag exists on
.BR payload
for packs only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.shroodlerrc
Optional YAML file of default flag values, loaded before command-line
parsing (command-line flags always win).
.TP
.I ~/.shroodler/history/
Default local scan-history store used by
.B history
and
.BR trend .
.TP
.I schema/finding.schema.json
The JSON Schema every crawl/payload output document is validated against.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error (network failure, bad input file, refused non-local target
without \fB\-\-allow\-external\fR). Re-run with
.B \-\-debug
for a full traceback.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (missing/invalid arguments), or no command given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Crawl a local target and write JSON:
.RS
.EX
shroodler crawl http://127.0.0.1:8081 -o out.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Crawl a remote target you're authorized to test, aggressively:
.RS
.EX
shroodler crawl https://example.com --allow-external --profile aggressive -o out.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Render an HTML report:
.RS
.EX
shroodler report out.json --format html -o out.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the active payload packs against discovered forms:
.RS
.EX
shroodler payload out.json -o hits.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Gate CI on new findings vs. a checked-in baseline:
.RS
.EX
shroodler diff out.json expected_findings.json --gate
.EE
.RE
.PP
Track a scan over time:
.RS
.EX
shroodler history record out.json --label nightly
shroodler trend <older-id> <newer-id>
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shroodler\-go (1),
.BR shroodler\-proxy (1)
.SH BUGS
Report issues at
.B https://github.com/Fantastic\-Fanta/Shroodler/issues .
